@startuml 02_what_is_atomic
title 2. What "atomic" means: no other thread can see it half done
skinparam backgroundColor #FFFFFF
skinparam roundcorner 12

rectangle "What makes an operation atomic?\nIt is either fully done or not started.\nNo other thread can observe it in between." as Def #FFF2CC

rectangle "x = x + 1 on a plain variable\nthree separate steps: load, add, store\nNOT safe when shared" as Plain #F8D7DA

rectangle "atomic load, then atomic store\neach access is safe on its own,\nbut the pair is not: lost updates\n(Experiment 1 negative control)" as LS #F8D7DA

rectangle "fetch_add(1)\none read-modify-write instruction: lock add\nSAFE" as FA #D4EDDA

rectangle "compare_exchange (CAS)\nread, compute, swap only if unchanged,\nretry if someone got there first\nSAFE" as CAS #D4EDDA

Def -down-> Plain
Def -down-> LS
Def -down-> FA
Def -down-> CAS

footer Atomics are used for counters, flags, reference counts, and as the building blocks of mutexes, spinlocks and lock-free queues.
@enduml
